<!-- SECTION 1 — CLASSICAL ERROR CORRECTION -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s1" class="section">
<div class="section-num">Section 01</div>
<h2>Classical Error Correction</h2>

<p>Whenever we send information through a channel — be it fiber optic cable, radio, or the internet — noise can flip bits. Error correction codes add <em>redundancy</em> to detect and fix these errors. Before tackling the quantum case, let's understand the classical approach.</p>

<div class="def">
  <div class="def-title">The Channel Model</div>
  We send a codeword c through a noisy channel. The receiver gets r = c &oplus; n, where n is the noise vector. Each bit n&#7522; = 1 (error) with probability p and n&#7522; = 0 (no error) with probability 1&minus;p.
  <div class="math-block" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
    <div class="eq">c &nbsp; → &nbsp; [Channel] &nbsp; → &nbsp; r = c ⊕ n</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The Repetition Code — The Simplest Code</h3>

<p>The simplest idea: repeat each bit multiple times. To transmit message bit m = 0, send the codeword c = (000). To transmit m = 1, send c = (111).</p>

<p>At the receiver, apply a <strong>majority rule</strong>: whatever value appears most often among the three received bits is the decoded message.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <style>
  .dec-table { border-collapse: collapse; font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.82rem; margin: 0.5rem 0; }
  .dec-table th { background: var(--ink); color: white; padding: 0.3rem 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .dec-table td { border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 0.25rem 0.8rem; text-align: center; }
  .dec-table .ok { color: var(--green); font-weight: 700; }
  .dec-table .err { color: var(--red); font-weight: 700; }
  </style>
  <table class="dec-table">
    <tr><th>Received r</th><th>Decoded</th><th>Correct?</th><th>Probability</th></tr>
    <tr><td>000</td><td>0</td><td class="ok">✓</td><td>(1&minus;p)&sup3;</td></tr>
    <tr><td>001</td><td>0</td><td class="ok">✓</td><td>(1&minus;p)&sup2;p</td></tr>
    <tr><td>010</td><td>0</td><td class="ok">✓</td><td>(1&minus;p)&sup2;p</td></tr>
    <tr><td>011</td><td>1</td><td class="err">✗</td><td>(1&minus;p)p&sup2;</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100</td><td>0</td><td class="ok">✓ (1 error corrected!)</td><td>(1&minus;p)&sup2;p</td></tr>
    <tr><td>101</td><td>1</td><td class="err">✗</td><td>(1&minus;p)p&sup2;</td></tr>
    <tr><td>110</td><td>1</td><td class="err">✗</td><td>(1&minus;p)p&sup2;</td></tr>
    <tr><td>111</td><td>1</td><td class="err">✗ (3 errors)</td><td>p&sup3;</td></tr>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">P<sub>err</sub> with code:</div><div class="mstep-body">= 3p&sup2;(1&minus;p) + p&sup3; &asymp; 3p&sup2; &nbsp;&nbsp; (for small p)</div></div>
  <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">P<sub>err</sub> without:</div><div class="mstep-body">= p</div></div>
  <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label" style="color:var(--green);">Improvement when:</div><div class="mstep-body">3p&sup2; &lt; p &nbsp;&hArr;&nbsp; p &lt; 1/3 &nbsp; &rarr; &nbsp; The code always helps if p &lt; 1/2!</div></div>
</div>

<div class="def">
  <div class="def-title">Code Notation [n, k, d]</div>
  A code that encodes k information bits into n physical bits and can correct up to t = &lfloor;(d&minus;1)/2&rfloor; errors is denoted [n, k, d]. The repetition code is a <strong>[3, 1, 3]</strong> code: n=3 bits, k=1 info bit, minimum distance d=3 (corrects t=1 error).
  <div class="math-block" style="margin-top:0.4rem;">
    <div class="eq">Rate &nbsp; R<sub>c</sub> = k/n &nbsp;&nbsp; (repetition code: R<sub>c</sub> = 1/3)</div>
    <div class="eq-note">&nbsp;&nbsp;Low rate is the cost of protection &mdash; 3 physical bits for 1 logical bit</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Interactive repetition code simulator -->
<canvas id="cvRepCode" width="560" height="200"></canvas>
<div class="canvas-caption">Classical repetition code — error probability comparison. Drag slider to change channel error rate p.</div>
<div class="controls">
  <label>Channel error rate p = <input type="range" id="slP" min="0" max="50" value="10" oninput="updateRepCode()"></label>
  <span class="ctrl-val" id="vP">0.10</span>
</div>
<div id="repInfo" style="font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:0.8rem;color:var(--accent);margin-top:0.4rem;"></div>

<!-- Exercise 1.1 -->
<div class="ex-block">
  <div class="ex-header"><span class="ex-tag">Ex 1.1</span><span class="ex-title">Error probability with the repetition code</span></div>
  <div class="ex-body">
    <p>The channel error probability is p = 0.1. What is the decoding error probability with the [3,1,3] repetition code? (Use P<sub>err</sub> = 3p&sup2;(1&minus;p) + p&sup3;.)</p>
  </div>
  <div class="answer-area">
    <div class="answer-row">
      <span class="ans-label">P<sub>err</sub> &asymp;</span>
      <input class="answer-input" id="a11" placeholder="e.g. 0.028" style="width:100px">
      <button class="btn sm" onclick="chkApprox('a11', 3*0.01*0.9+0.001, 'fb11')">Check</button>
      <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="showHint('h11')">show hint</button>
    </div>
    <div class="feedback hint" id="h11">3 × (0.1)² × (0.9) + (0.1)³ = 3 × 0.01 × 0.9 + 0.001 = 0.027 + 0.001 = 0.028.</div>
    <div class="feedback" id="fb11"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Exercise 1.2 -->
<div class="ex-block">
  <div class="ex-header"><span class="ex-tag">Ex 1.2</span><span class="ex-title">When does the code help?</span></div>
  <div class="ex-body">
    <p>For what values of p does the repetition code actually reduce error probability? (Compare 3p² < p.)</p>
  </div>
  <div class="answer-area">
    <div class="answer-row">
      <span class="ans-label">p &lt;</span>
      <input class="answer-input" id="a12" placeholder="fraction" style="width:80px">
      <button class="btn sm" onclick="chkApprox('a12', 1/3, 'fb12')">Check</button>
    </div>
    <div class="feedback hint" id="h12">3p² &lt; p → 3p &lt; 1 → p &lt; 1/3.</div>
    <div class="feedback" id="fb12"></div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

